========================================================================================
 Credential lease visibility: Azure, AWS, Database secret engines
========================================================================================
 The question this answers: for every leased, expiring credential Vault currently has
 issued, when was it created, and how many days until it expires.

 STATUS: verified 2026-09-03 against a real Vault, a real Postgres backend, and real
 issued leases. All 6 searches below executed against a live Splunk instance and
 returned rows (or, for LV.5, a verified-correct empty result, see its own note).

 WHY THIS IS NOT A SEARCH OVER index=vault_audit, AND CANNOT BE:
 The Vault audit log never records a credential's expiry, at any layer. Verified: the
 credential-issuance audit event has no lease_duration or expire_time field at all, only
 a clear-text lease_id. The one API call that does return expiry (sys/leases/lookup)
 HMACs its own lease_id in the audit log, both as input and output, so it cannot be
 joined back to the clear-text lease_id from issuance. Role TTL config is HMAC'd too.
 Expiry is simply not recoverable from the audit stream by any join.

 What DOES work: polling Vault's live API directly. scripts/vault-lease-inventory.py
 does this (LIST sys/leases/lookup/<mount>/creds/<role>/, then look up each lease ID for
 its real issue_time/expire_time/ttl) and writes one JSON line per active lease, per
 poll, to a file this index tails. Run it on a schedule: leases expire between polls, a
 one-time snapshot goes stale within the shortest TTL in your estate.

 ENGINE COVERAGE: only `database` has been tested end to end. `aws` and `azure` are
 wired into the poller on the assumption they follow the same shape (both issue under
 <mount>/creds/<role>, which is Vault's documented default), but that is UNVERIFIED.
 Every row carries verified_engine=true/false; do not present an unverified row with the
 same confidence as a verified one.

 REPEATED-POLL DATA: this file is appended on every poll, so it accumulates history.
 Every search below filters to the LATEST poll only, using
     | eventstats max(polled_at) AS latest | where polled_at=latest
 not the SPL `map` command. `map`'s `$field$` token substitution collides with Simple
 XML dashboard token syntax (they use the identical $name$ form), which makes a
 dashboard panel built on `map` hang forever showing "Search is waiting for input..."
 even though no input is actually missing. Found by running the dashboard, not by
 reading the SPL. Do not reintroduce `map` into a panel search in this file.

 Assumes the poller output loaded as index=vault_leases sourcetype=vault:lease:snapshot.
========================================================================================


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 LV.0  Sanity check. Run this first.
       If total is 0, either nothing has been polled yet, or the monitor input is not
       pointed at the right file. If unverified_rows is nonzero, some of what follows
       is from an engine type this has never been proven against.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_leases sourcetype=vault:lease:snapshot
| stats count AS total,
        sum(eval(if(verified_engine="false",1,0))) AS unverified_rows,
        max(polled_at) AS last_poll
| eval last_poll = strftime(last_poll, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 LV.1  The current answer: latest poll only, one row per still-active lease
       A file appended on every poll accumulates history. Reading it raw would show
       every past poll's rows too, which makes an expired lease from an hour ago look
       like it is still active. eventstats+where restricts to the latest poll.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_leases sourcetype=vault:lease:snapshot
| eventstats max(polled_at) AS latest
| where polled_at=latest
| table mount role lease_id engine_type verified_engine days_to_expiry bucket renewable issue_time expire_time
| eval issue_time=strftime(issue_time,"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"), expire_time=strftime(expire_time,"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")
| sort + days_to_expiry


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 LV.2  Headline counts, latest poll only
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_leases sourcetype=vault:lease:snapshot
| eventstats max(polled_at) AS latest
| where polled_at=latest
| stats count AS active_leases,
        sum(eval(if(bucket="1 <=1h",1,0))) AS expiring_within_1h,
        dc(mount) AS mounts_with_active_leases


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 LV.3  By mount and role, latest poll only
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_leases sourcetype=vault:lease:snapshot
| eventstats max(polled_at) AS latest
| where polled_at=latest
| stats count AS active, avg(days_to_expiry) AS avg_days_to_expiry BY mount role
| eval avg_days_to_expiry = round(avg_days_to_expiry, 2)
| sort - active


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 LV.4  Expiry trend across polls (needs the poller run on a schedule)
       Uses every poll in range, deliberately, unlike LV.1-LV.3. Falls back to a single
       point if only one poll has ever run. That is expected on a fresh install, not a
       broken search.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_leases sourcetype=vault:lease:snapshot
| eval poll_time = strftime(polled_at, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M")
| stats count AS active_leases,
        sum(eval(if(bucket="1 <=1h",1,0))) AS expiring_soon
        BY poll_time
| sort poll_time


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 LV.5  Which engines have never been verified, and how much of the estate they cover,
       latest poll only.
       If this returns rows, some fraction of what LV.1-LV.3 report is built on an
       assumption (aws/azure follow the same shape as database) rather than a proof.
       Say so out loud before presenting those numbers as fact. Verified 2026-09-03 to
       correctly return EMPTY when only the database engine has ever been polled.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_leases sourcetype=vault:lease:snapshot verified_engine=false
| eventstats max(polled_at) AS latest
| where polled_at=latest
| stats count AS unverified_leases BY engine_type mount
